  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Strengthened labour market information system  for evidence based policy design and decision-making</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The National Statistics Office (NSO) has, within its mandate been updating the Labour Market information. The main objective is to collect basic statistics on the labour force situation in Malawi for making informed decision that aim at improving the employment situation of the Zambian citizenly. The indicators provide for monitoring and evaluating progress made towards the attainment of national development goals and global commitments with an overarching goal of promoting the welfare of the Malawian population while ensuring the availability of labour force indicators to feed into Malawi Vision 2063 and the Mid Term Plan (10 year development strategy) Ministerial Strategic Plans, Budget briefs, among others.
Therefore, the Country Programme Outcome is supporting the constituents to produce and analyse quality statistics using innovative methods based on sound internationally agreed statistical standards adopted by the ICLS, and to strengthen its capacity to compile and disseminate harmonized data on SDG indicators and emerging new areas of labour statistics with the more disaggregated scope, particularly focusing on gender, groups in vulnerable situations and specific economic sectors. The emergence of new forms of work and associated characteristics, as well as recently adopted ILO Conventions, call on the ILO to provide adequate statistical definitions and guidance to be eventually adopted as an international statistical standard by the International Conference for Labour Statisticians (ICLS).
Beneficiaries of this intervention include: Government, the private sector, the academia and the general public for making informed decision that aim at improving the employment situation of the Zambian citizenly.
The intervention is collaborating with the Government of Malawi through the National Statistical Office, Ministry of Labour, African Union, UN agencies and other cooperating partners.</narrative>
  </description>
